#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550057} :activity/id #uuid "be2f0019-a161-5405-a7a2-e77856e00af4" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18510c8b-e669-5011-aa0f-5f9e17e08d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333206} {:answer/id #uuid "756ded08-1584-5e69-aa8a-0f9ea158b9a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334049} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7c7fd3-e1ab-54db-88ef-745e0c891aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334526} {:answer/id #uuid "af3b9b11-3257-5dca-a1de-29e8d4614542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{7t}-2t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334533} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b7cca8-117d-5882-8808-4eb7ab006ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{7t}-4t{e}^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334878}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334526} :answer-field/id #uuid "853ffbce-93a2-5e36-a152-729501632660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41fb4ba9-2a2a-520b-a6ad-5cdc344e0ff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-64798" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-14{y}^{′}+49y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+49{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{7t}$ and $t{e}^{7t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}$ is not a solution."} :step/id #uuid "24101b53-04f5-5a42-abcd-bf23100ae66d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00390579-73db-58de-8e3f-f3ca75d10676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549320} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca1573d-75dd-57d1-975d-20b4eabf81d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549523} {:answer/id #uuid "76eeaeb4-2ab6-520a-b6b0-9a2f15095f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549544} {:answer/id #uuid "a24492a0-f30c-5760-a699-262c2d5c1e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549624} {:answer/id #uuid "b388bc7a-f341-596b-93dd-60183e4ec2b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549624} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5917ecc-743e-5b98-928c-b4e265150550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2595de8f-014d-5db6-a5d3-e22363b7564e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152903" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[9X+4].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{2},\\,0<x<2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[9X+4]=9\\text{E}[X]+4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[9X+4] & =9\\text{E}[X]+4 \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "37f56bed-06b2-5321-bd27-98c4d186cd0e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12276898-9979-57a0-8ed0-2ba59a641ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨3,2⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263170} {:answer/id #uuid "154fa3a3-16f8-55a0-846e-51231182efb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨3,3⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263198} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4bb37a-bda2-5a72-a223-f0c5b9c34121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨2,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264193} {:answer/id #uuid "c17bef72-8988-5da9-b2e6-8b78f6621cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264680} {:answer/id #uuid "f580560e-dc14-5b52-a0f5-5aaae87a83f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264680} :answer-field/id #uuid "60641db1-7e26-5e36-8c32-48b2fb158832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "256d5ce8-b202-5957-9fe3-266dd8fdafa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132208" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩+⟨-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "580422bf-3d6c-5210-b285-aa8671ab0768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249e501c-2f96-5d3a-aca1-a06ed673e1ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 5 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549393} {:answer/id #uuid "2592d63c-e1be-5dbf-bf09-9ff0d9f2f561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,11 & 8 \\\\ \\,-28 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549394} {:answer/id #uuid "41433e06-ff80-564d-9ce3-d0dc9852a8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549448} {:answer/id #uuid "752e27ae-6bc8-5acc-bb2a-b47134fc80d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549538} {:answer/id #uuid "8a477f06-b877-5fa0-bce3-9f5744ae6971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & \\,-28 \\\\ 2 & \\,11\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549538} :answer-field/id #uuid "8429c5f3-10e5-5e89-82ae-2a624d986bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc096f25-da43-5ef0-8dca-9310fea097c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-42981" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7b187b67-e213-5cbc-9db5-c90f4b06f5c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e9cdc8-9841-5f0d-ab5a-8833d16224d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127100} {:answer/id #uuid "93552b22-934c-541e-af6f-55949ee3eb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127101} {:answer/id #uuid "ad44e94e-055c-5eb9-af28-414c9a6aff32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127102} {:answer/id #uuid "703cf7cc-9888-5857-b179-d931e8128941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127103} {:answer/id #uuid "70751244-3954-52d4-81d1-a9cbbad5fd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127104}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fe156952-6c9e-51ed-a0eb-ed79cba1476f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127099} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b22824-daf4-5d0e-b5e9-203cb0249071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382041} {:answer/id #uuid "8033de03-7e63-5b27-9a40-3b6ef01194d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382042} {:answer/id #uuid "de1a6139-5d33-5664-9017-a573aaa19ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382043} {:answer/id #uuid "33af0c6d-3453-5b4f-868b-dc798d347c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382044} {:answer/id #uuid "30ea66e6-af09-5377-9e27-ac3aee6ef9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382045} :answer-field/id #uuid "452b2833-f8aa-57bb-843b-3f3e95179f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fcfe0e97-f702-5d83-9f81-8d21ad944be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-36949" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7(\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=9,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "916157f9-0c04-5b62-b14f-2a351f6e1133" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d13c75-e6bc-5e2e-a8a5-6dd560e659ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549354} {:answer/id #uuid "209da59d-2b86-550d-89c6-5385f915b5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549381} {:answer/id #uuid "570944f0-ed2d-54ac-afa7-c23ef2d1f070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549492} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe4a7d2-be73-53f9-b806-31ea43cf2ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549678} {:answer/id #uuid "d70dba8c-62c5-5199-93bd-0c9dd94ff141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549381} :answer-field/id #uuid "daee834d-972a-5370-8934-102b16e2168b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "15721919-affc-527a-806f-d54311da77ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-97580" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7d92401-cb3f-5281-8499-b4adbba3d575" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550077}}] :activity/title "Quiz 4" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549312}]}
